## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical ability to extend a docker-dca setup with a compliant, low-latency data-redaction path.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config-driven per-tenant policies
- Sidecar pattern and in-memory caching
- Streaming JSON redaction without full buffering
- Nginx-edge integration and TLS context

## Code Example
```go
package main
import (
  "encoding/json"
  "fmt"
)
func redact(m map[string]interface{}, fields map[string]bool){
  for k,v := range m {
    if fields[k] { m[k] = "***REDACTED***"; continue }
    if mm, ok := v.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(mm, fields) }
    if arr, ok := v.([]interface{}); ok {
      for _, it := range arr { if sub, ok := it.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(sub, fields) } }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test nested JSON redaction with large bodies?
- How would you monitor redaction performance without affecting latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to enforce data residency and leakage controls at runtime with low impact, using Vault for policy and identity, and blue/green rollouts for safe deployments.

## Key Concepts
- Data-loss prevention in containerized traffic
- Vault integration and SPIFFE SVID rotation
- Tenant-scoped JSON schemas
- Dynamic policy updates and hot-reload
- Canary/Blue-green deployment patterns

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal pseudo sidecar validator
const Validator = require('jsonschema').Validator;
const v = new Validator();
const tenant = process.env.TENANT_HEADER; // from request header
const schema = fetchVaultSchema(tenant); // resolves /secret/tenants/{tenant}/schema
v.addSchema(schema, '/tenant/schema');
const payload = getOutboundBody();
const result = v.validate(payload, { // simplified schema
  type: 'object', properties: { tenant: {type: 'string'}, data: {type: 'object'} }, required: ['tenant','data']
}, '/tenant/schema');
if (!result.valid) blockOrRedact(payload, result.errors);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test false positives/negatives per tenant?
- How would you handle schema evolution without breaking existing tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic, per-tenant tracing in a multi-DC docker-dca setup, including config retrieval, TTL-based caching, and graceful degradation when the sidecar cannot fetch config.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry sampling and per-tenant configuration
- Consul KV as a central per-tenant policy store
- TTL caching to limit external calls
- Graceful fallback to sensible default rate
- Cross-DC deployment considerations and observability impact

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-sampler for per-tenant tracing
class TenantSampler {
  constructor(fetchRate) {
    this.fetchRate = fetchRate; // function(tenant) -> rate
    this.cache = new Map(); // tenant -> {rate, expiry}
  }
  getRate(tenant) {
    const entry = this.cache.get(tenant);
    if (entry && entry.expiry > Date.now()) return entry.rate;
    // fetch from Consul KV here, then cache for 5s
    const rate = this.fetchRate(tenant) ?? 20;
    this.cache.set(tenant, { rate, expiry: Date.now() + 5000 });
    return rate;
  }
  shouldSample(tenant) {
    const rate = this.getRate(tenant);
    return Math.random() * 100 < rate;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness of per-tenant rates during rollout?
- What are failure modes if Consul KV is unavailable, and how to mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design distributed feature-flag propagation with per-tenant isolation across data centers. It covers Docker Config for tenant flags, per-tenant in-process caching with TTL, Redis Pub/Sub for invalidation and cross-DC propagation, and edge security with TLS termination at Traefik.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for tenant-specific flag storage
- Per-tenant in-process cache with configurable TTL
- Redis Pub/Sub for cache invalidation and propagation
- Lightweight policy/flag service coordinating across DCs
- TLS termination at Traefik edge proxy

## Code Example
```javascript
class FlagCache {
  constructor(redis, ttlSeconds = 120) {
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.ttlSeconds = ttlSeconds;
    this.redis = redis;

    redis.subscribe('tenant-flags', (err, count) => {
      if (err) console.error('Subscribe error:', err);
    });

    redis.on('message', (channel, message) => {
      if (channel === 'tenant-flags') {
        const { tenantId } = JSON.parse(message);
        this.cache.delete(tenantId);
      }
    });
  }

  async getFlag(tenantId, feature) {
    const key = `${tenantId}:${feature}`;
    const entry = this.cache.get(key);

    if (entry && Date.now() < entry.expiresAt) {
      return entry.value;
    }

    const value = await this.redis.hget(`flags:${tenantId}`, feature);
    this.cache.set(key, { value: JSON.parse(value), expiresAt: Date.now() + this.ttlSeconds * 1000 });
    return JSON.parse(value);
  }
}
```
